1
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:23,600
Amphibians are ancient creatures,
living fossils in a modern world.

2
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:30,480
But frogs, toads and salamanders
are more diverse,

3
00:00:30,679 --> 00:00:33,880
colourful and creative
than you could ever imagine.

4
00:00:45,079 --> 00:00:48,359
Whether dodging danger,
fighting rivals,

5
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:52,320
raising their young
or wearing a disguise,

6
00:00:52,399 --> 00:00:54,399
amphibians are awesome.

7
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:03,000
So hop along to your local pond,
dive in

8
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:08,040
and explore the fascinating world
of the frogs and their cousins.

9
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:42,840
Dark, steaming, ancient forest.
Like a land that time forgot.

10
00:01:53,680 --> 00:01:56,319
A monster
lurking beneath the surface.

11
00:01:58,680 --> 00:02:00,760
It evolved in the Jurassic period.

12
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:08,840
This human-sized beast could have
been dodging the feet of dinosaurs.

13
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:14,120
But this is today.
A giant salamander.

14
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:19,280
It can hunt in darkness
by sensing prey.

15
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:21,840
It can breathe
through its skin underwater.

16
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:25,680
It can regrow limbs,
produce toxic slime

17
00:02:25,879 --> 00:02:29,120
and live for nearly a century.

18
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:34,400
It's the crowning glory
of the amphibian family.

19
00:02:42,280 --> 00:02:44,400
At school, every child is taught

20
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:48,560
that amphibians bridge the gap
between fish and reptiles.

21
00:02:56,599 --> 00:02:59,280
Fish must live in water,
breed in water

22
00:02:59,439 --> 00:03:03,000
and breathe oxygen from the water
through their gills.

23
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:16,120
Reptiles have adapted
to life on land.

24
00:03:16,560 --> 00:03:19,280
They have scaly skin,
breathe with lungs

25
00:03:19,560 --> 00:03:23,000
and most lay shelled eggs,
always on land.

26
00:03:36,400 --> 00:03:38,680
Amphibians are totally unique.

27
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:44,439
Their name means 'both lives'.
They have mastered land and water.

28
00:03:48,280 --> 00:03:51,840
They bridge both worlds
and can breathe air with lungs

29
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:54,840
or oxygen from water
through their skin.

30
00:03:56,840 --> 00:04:00,280
But unlike reptiles,
they must lay their eggs in water

31
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:03,719
as they don't have hard shells
and could dry out.

32
00:04:05,840 --> 00:04:07,599
Egg laying is quite a palaver ...

33
00:04:08,599 --> 00:04:11,400
and all that most of us
see of our slimy neighbours.

34
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:16,720
Spring is in the air.

35
00:04:18,040 --> 00:04:20,279
And the forest is buzzing.

36
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:25,040
There's a party,
and everyone's invited.

37
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:31,040
When European moor frogs are ready
to mate, they make quite a splash.

38
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:38,240
They're a locally abundant species.

39
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:42,600
Several hundred can rub shoulders
in every hectare of wetland.

40
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:48,279
But now they cram more in
from far and wide.

41
00:04:48,480 --> 00:04:53,279
Up to 20 per square metre,
in a giant amphibian orgy.

42
00:04:57,040 --> 00:05:01,240
The usually grey-brown males
flush ghostly blue in excitement.

43
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:05,040
They're looking
for available females

44
00:05:05,240 --> 00:05:08,720
and don't intend to let one slip
through their fingers.

45
00:05:10,360 --> 00:05:12,480
Their thumbs have nuptial pads,

46
00:05:12,720 --> 00:05:16,920
rough areas, to give them a better
grip on their slippery lovers.

47
00:05:20,920 --> 00:05:24,040
When a female is spotted,
the contest begins.

48
00:05:27,720 --> 00:05:31,240
The male's endless chirping
and splashing draws an audience.

49
00:05:38,240 --> 00:05:42,480
Master of stealth, the bittern is
a well-camouflaged heron.

50
00:05:48,399 --> 00:05:50,920
Patience is his middle-name.

51
00:05:58,480 --> 00:06:01,240
It's a waiting game
that usually pays off.

52
00:06:14,160 --> 00:06:16,720
Ever heard the expression
'frog in the throat'?

53
00:06:19,279 --> 00:06:23,240
Turns out wriggling frog
isn't the easiest thing to swallow.

54
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:33,480
It doesn't spoil
the party atmosphere though.

55
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:36,720
A successful male won't let go,

56
00:06:36,920 --> 00:06:40,040
despite the best attempts of others
to dislodge him.

57
00:06:42,360 --> 00:06:45,839
He needs to be in position
as the female releases her eggs.

58
00:06:46,040 --> 00:06:50,240
In perfect synchrony, he pumps out
sperm over the mounds of jelly.

59
00:06:53,240 --> 00:06:56,800
Each female can release anywhere
from a whopping 500 eggs

60
00:06:57,040 --> 00:07:00,240
to an eye-watering three thousand!

61
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:03,720
The eggs are small but expand,

62
00:07:03,839 --> 00:07:07,800
absorbing water to become the more
familiar mounds of frog spawn.

63
00:07:11,399 --> 00:07:13,600
The sheer numbers seem excessive,

64
00:07:13,720 --> 00:07:17,240
but the amphibian world
is constantly under attack.

65
00:07:19,720 --> 00:07:23,279
This time a grey heron
invites itself to the party.

66
00:07:29,240 --> 00:07:32,600
Frogs need large litters
to keep the population topped up.

67
00:07:36,600 --> 00:07:38,600
So they keep at it.

68
00:07:39,360 --> 00:07:42,480
They only breed for 20 days
or so each spring

69
00:07:42,720 --> 00:07:45,240
and must make the most
of the narrow window.

70
00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:49,160
All of their energy
goes into the next generation.

71
00:07:50,160 --> 00:07:52,240
Then an amphibian miracle.

72
00:07:52,480 --> 00:07:56,160
Each perfect globe undergoes
an incredible transformation.

73
00:08:19,720 --> 00:08:22,800
The tiny larvae
wriggle free of their jelly mould.

74
00:08:25,839 --> 00:08:28,040
There's not much
family resemblance.

75
00:08:28,240 --> 00:08:31,800
Unlike the adults,
they extract oxygen from the water

76
00:08:32,080 --> 00:08:34,080
through fingery gills.

77
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:40,440
They munch only
on microscopic algae.

78
00:08:43,720 --> 00:08:46,240
But there's amphibian magic
on its way.

79
00:08:46,480 --> 00:08:51,759
Over a few short weeks they undergo
a spectacular metamorphosis.

80
00:08:56,519 --> 00:08:59,720
They start adding
tiny water creatures like daphnia,

81
00:08:59,879 --> 00:09:02,360
the water flea, to their diet.

82
00:09:02,639 --> 00:09:06,480
These minute crustaceans
are the basis of the food chain,

83
00:09:06,639 --> 00:09:09,519
and are even eaten
by the plants themselves,

84
00:09:09,720 --> 00:09:13,240
such as the carnivorous
utricularia!

85
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:17,000
The pond
offers a buffet of mini-meals.

86
00:09:17,600 --> 00:09:20,519
As adult frogs
they won't eat plants at all.

87
00:09:20,720 --> 00:09:23,840
Their life of carnivory
starts here.

88
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,519
Eventually
they'll absorb their own tails,

89
00:09:27,720 --> 00:09:31,480
recycling the nutrients
into characteristic hoping legs.

90
00:09:37,120 --> 00:09:39,480
The tadpole stage
lasts less than a month,

91
00:09:39,720 --> 00:09:42,480
but it will be several years
until the frogs are big enough

92
00:09:42,639 --> 00:09:45,240
to return for an orgy of their own.

93
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:52,600
The amphibians, frogs, toads and
salamanders, are pretty amazing.

94
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:57,720
And they've got
a long track record of success.

95
00:10:02,480 --> 00:10:05,120
The first amphibians
crawled from primal swamps

96
00:10:05,360 --> 00:10:08,120
around 370 million years ago.

97
00:10:13,480 --> 00:10:18,000
At that time, sea levels
were falling, exposing new land.

98
00:10:19,840 --> 00:10:22,720
Some fish,
similar to modern lungfish,

99
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:27,720
were experimenting with leg-like
fins and the first proto-type lungs

100
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:30,480
so that they could explore
the shores.

101
00:10:38,759 --> 00:10:41,120
If a dry season
took away their pools,

102
00:10:41,240 --> 00:10:43,879
they could survive
for short periods on land,

103
00:10:44,120 --> 00:10:46,240
and even 'walk' to find water.

104
00:10:47,840 --> 00:10:51,480
They were the first vertebrates
to ever venture above the surface.

105
00:10:53,519 --> 00:10:58,480
A fashion that soon caught on.
It was the start of something big.

106
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:04,000
In these great-grandfathers
of amphibians,

107
00:11:04,120 --> 00:11:06,879
fins gradually became fully
functional legs

108
00:11:07,120 --> 00:11:11,000
that could support the body,
and gills disappeared altogether,

109
00:11:11,240 --> 00:11:14,759
replaced by bigger
and more efficient lungs.

110
00:11:17,240 --> 00:11:19,519
What was one small step for a fish,

111
00:11:19,720 --> 00:11:22,720
was a giant leap
for amphibian kind.

112
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:32,000
It opened up a whole new world.

113
00:11:35,120 --> 00:11:39,120
At that time, the only animals
were invertebrates.

114
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,360
A bug buffet.

115
00:11:50,600 --> 00:11:54,120
and the amphibians
were the first to the feast.

116
00:12:06,360 --> 00:12:10,240
In a humid, lush rainforest
landscape they thrived.

117
00:12:10,360 --> 00:12:13,480
But the climate was changing,
drying.

118
00:12:20,480 --> 00:12:23,759
They were left behind
when reptiles, and later, mammals,

119
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,240
started to dominate the scene.

120
00:12:32,519 --> 00:12:35,879
The damp places of the world
remained their domain.

121
00:12:40,360 --> 00:12:43,519
By the Jurassic,
when dinosaurs ruled the Earth,

122
00:12:43,720 --> 00:12:47,240
amphibians looked more or less
the same as they do today.

123
00:12:54,360 --> 00:12:56,480
If it ain't broke, don't fix it!

124
00:13:03,480 --> 00:13:07,480
Today
there are around 7,000 species.

125
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:16,240
The smallest are just
a few millimetres long,

126
00:13:17,480 --> 00:13:20,360
the biggest is the size of a human.

127
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:28,240
Under the cover of darkness,
the monster hunts.

128
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:35,120
It's nearly two metres long, giant,
but dwarfed by ancient relatives.

129
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:40,000
Fossils reveal
ancient amphibian cousins,

130
00:13:41,240 --> 00:13:44,240
like Prionosuchus,
nine metres long.

131
00:13:49,240 --> 00:13:54,000
A slow metabolism means it can go
for weeks without feeding,

132
00:13:54,120 --> 00:13:58,639
but when it does hunt, it ambushes
fish and smaller amphibians...

133
00:14:00,120 --> 00:14:03,120
using a vacuum to try
and draw them into its mouth.

134
00:14:05,759 --> 00:14:10,360
This species has barely changed
for 160 million years.

135
00:14:11,759 --> 00:14:14,600
But their cousins
have diversified with style.

136
00:14:19,759 --> 00:14:23,000
Ninety percent of the world's
amphibians are frogs!

137
00:14:24,639 --> 00:14:27,120
Their success is thanks
to the tools of their trade,

138
00:14:28,120 --> 00:14:30,639
not least their iconic frog legs.

139
00:14:33,240 --> 00:14:35,240
These are not just menu items,

140
00:14:35,600 --> 00:14:39,720
but a masterful way of travelling
efficiently and dodging danger.

141
00:14:42,519 --> 00:14:46,360
Before they leap, the frogs
stretch their muscles and tendons,

142
00:14:46,480 --> 00:14:50,600
generating mechanical energy
that catapults them forwards.

143
00:15:00,360 --> 00:15:04,480
Some are so good at it
that they seem to walk on water.

144
00:15:13,360 --> 00:15:17,759
Frogs aren't the biggest creatures.
For them it's a dangerous world.

145
00:15:21,360 --> 00:15:24,480
Wherever they roam,
they need to keep on hopping.

146
00:15:27,240 --> 00:15:31,240
When they're not hopping to escape,
they can do it in pursuit of food,

147
00:15:31,480 --> 00:15:34,120
utilising another frog-tastic tool.

148
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,600
A lightning-fast sticky tongue.

149
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,759
Like high speed Velcro,
it's thrown at the victim.

150
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:50,240
The fleshy end wraps around the
bug, coating it with sticky saliva.

151
00:15:53,840 --> 00:15:55,879
Forget 'blink and you'll miss it'.

152
00:15:56,120 --> 00:16:00,120
A frog could strike out five times
before your eyelids were open.

153
00:16:11,720 --> 00:16:14,720
The biggest tongue
belongs to the bull frog.

154
00:16:14,879 --> 00:16:16,600
It's a good fly swat ...

155
00:16:18,360 --> 00:16:21,360
that can even be used
to catch birds.

156
00:16:25,720 --> 00:16:28,759
Then its big eye balls
help shove it down its throat.

157
00:16:31,639 --> 00:16:34,759
Though sometimes its eyes
are bigger than its stomach.

158
00:16:43,840 --> 00:16:45,840
An emperor moth caterpillar.

159
00:16:46,240 --> 00:16:49,519
It's binge-eating,
loading its body with nutrients,

160
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:53,000
ready to transform
into an adult moth.

161
00:16:55,600 --> 00:16:59,000
A six centimetre
succulent sack of protein.

162
00:17:00,120 --> 00:17:03,600
But for a European tree frog
there's a problem.

163
00:17:05,759 --> 00:17:07,759
The bug is bigger than he is.

164
00:17:10,160 --> 00:17:13,000
And it's covered
with irritating hairs.

165
00:17:19,240 --> 00:17:21,240
Time to jump ship.

166
00:17:25,480 --> 00:17:28,759
Generally, the ravenous bug-eating
appetites of amphibians

167
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:33,720
benefit others: they chow down on
pests, controlling their numbers.

168
00:17:36,480 --> 00:17:38,240
But they don't discriminate,

169
00:17:38,480 --> 00:17:41,960
and sometimes useful animals
also end up on the menu.

170
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:48,559
The world would be buried in poop
were it not for poop-eaters.

171
00:17:52,240 --> 00:17:54,480
Dung beetles are tireless workers

172
00:17:54,559 --> 00:17:57,720
who dedicate their lives
to cleaning up after others.

173
00:17:58,240 --> 00:18:00,279
It's not enough
to win them respect.

174
00:18:02,240 --> 00:18:05,559
They feed on dung and collect it
to lay their eggs in.

175
00:18:06,480 --> 00:18:08,960
An edible nursery for their young.

176
00:18:13,720 --> 00:18:16,240
In fact, dung gets them in the mood

177
00:18:16,480 --> 00:18:18,720
as they begin
their family planning.

178
00:18:21,720 --> 00:18:25,720
But it's hard to find privacy, away
from the prying eyes of a toad.

179
00:18:29,519 --> 00:18:34,000
Amphibians can't chew their food,
hence their generous stomachs.

180
00:18:37,240 --> 00:18:40,240
Their multi-functional mouths
are musical too.

181
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:51,480
When the tree frogs blow up
their balloons to impress a mate

182
00:18:51,720 --> 00:18:54,720
they prefer to do it
under the safe cover of darkness.

183
00:19:06,960 --> 00:19:09,319
Frogs have simple ears
that are well designed

184
00:19:09,519 --> 00:19:12,000
to pick up the high frequency
love songs.

185
00:19:12,240 --> 00:19:14,720
But others sometimes listen in.

186
00:19:16,720 --> 00:19:18,240
Fire bellied toads,

187
00:19:18,480 --> 00:19:22,240
so-called for the dazzling orange
blotches on their undersides

188
00:19:22,319 --> 00:19:24,720
put-their-all
into their performance.

189
00:19:37,480 --> 00:19:39,720
To sing they pump themselves
full of air

190
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:43,039
and shift it back and forth
through their vocal sacs.

191
00:19:45,319 --> 00:19:47,480
It means they're very buoyant!

192
00:19:52,480 --> 00:19:56,759
It's such an impressive display
that the odd frog gets lured in.

193
00:20:09,799 --> 00:20:13,240
After some confused fumbling,
they get back to business.

194
00:20:20,720 --> 00:20:23,720
But there's a downside
to his performance strategy.

195
00:20:27,480 --> 00:20:31,559
Floaters are easy to spot
and vulnerable to attack.

196
00:20:35,559 --> 00:20:39,240
The toads produce a venom that can
irritate the throats of anything

197
00:20:39,480 --> 00:20:42,720
that tries to eat them,
but it doesn't always work.

198
00:20:46,240 --> 00:20:48,559
The flood meadows
they use for their displays

199
00:20:48,759 --> 00:20:52,000
are a favoured feeding ground
for migratory birds.

200
00:21:01,519 --> 00:21:05,000
One male sees the intruder
and puts on a different show,

201
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,240
curling to show his orange patches,

202
00:21:08,319 --> 00:21:11,000
a warning
that he won't taste nice.

203
00:21:16,240 --> 00:21:18,480
And it seems to do the trick.

204
00:21:22,240 --> 00:21:24,759
Each coupling
results in up to 300 eggs

205
00:21:25,799 --> 00:21:28,000
and they too
will face a life of peril.

206
00:21:43,480 --> 00:21:46,960
But frogs and toads have many
tricks to help them through.

207
00:21:55,480 --> 00:21:58,720
The famous slimy skin of amphibians
is a winner.

208
00:22:00,240 --> 00:22:02,319
They can breathe through it
in water.

209
00:22:03,319 --> 00:22:05,559
But can cope on dry land.

210
00:22:11,240 --> 00:22:13,480
It makes them weather-proof.

211
00:22:17,480 --> 00:22:20,000
And can provide cryptic camo.

212
00:22:25,720 --> 00:22:29,559
The skin makes its own slime from
mucous glands to keep is moist.

213
00:22:31,519 --> 00:22:35,000
And in some species
the glands also produce poisons.

214
00:22:37,480 --> 00:22:38,799
Along the ears of toads,

215
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:43,480
the glands contain a neurotoxin
called bufotoxin or frog toxin.

216
00:22:49,240 --> 00:22:52,319
Many poisonous species
advertise their talent,

217
00:22:52,519 --> 00:22:55,480
but few with the flare
of the fire salamander.

218
00:23:01,240 --> 00:23:03,240
It might have misjudged the stream,

219
00:23:03,480 --> 00:23:05,799
but there's no mistaking
the warning signs.

220
00:23:09,240 --> 00:23:11,720
Black and yellow
indicate poison glands

221
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,799
that run head to tail
along both sides of his body.

222
00:23:22,039 --> 00:23:24,480
This little salamander
is a creature of habit,

223
00:23:24,720 --> 00:23:26,480
he likes to spend his time
travelling around

224
00:23:26,720 --> 00:23:30,720
just 60 square meters
of damp forest that he calls home.

225
00:23:34,720 --> 00:23:39,000
He has favoured hiding places that
he'll use regularly for decades.

226
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:45,480
And as he patrols, he's constantly
on the lookout for food.

227
00:24:02,720 --> 00:24:05,240
An earthworm
is hard to get to grips with.

228
00:24:13,480 --> 00:24:17,960
Amphibian kind don't chew their
food, it's straight down the hatch.

229
00:24:28,960 --> 00:24:31,000
He can wander boldly
through the forest

230
00:24:31,240 --> 00:24:33,240
thanks to his chemical defence.

231
00:24:42,960 --> 00:24:46,720
He produces alkaloid toxins
that can cause muscle contractions

232
00:24:46,960 --> 00:24:49,480
and hyperventilation
in other animals.

233
00:24:49,720 --> 00:24:52,480
It also kills
fungal and bacterial infections,

234
00:24:52,720 --> 00:24:55,039
keeping his skin
healthy and bright.

235
00:25:04,279 --> 00:25:08,079
There are more than 650 salamanders
in the world.

236
00:25:11,039 --> 00:25:15,559
All have toxins to keep away biting
insects and stop fungal infections,

237
00:25:15,759 --> 00:25:17,759
but they're not all deadly.

238
00:25:37,279 --> 00:25:39,720
Some of the biggest
come out at night.

239
00:25:50,720 --> 00:25:52,799
It looks like the stuff
of nightmares,

240
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:56,799
but North America's hellbender
does not live up to its name.

241
00:26:00,759 --> 00:26:05,000
It's a more primitive model,
with gill slits and no eyelids.

242
00:26:08,960 --> 00:26:11,319
Lurking in the shadows, he hunts.

243
00:26:19,960 --> 00:26:22,480
A more likeable cousin
is on the move.

244
00:26:25,480 --> 00:26:28,559
One group of small salamanders
is known as the newts,

245
00:26:30,039 --> 00:26:32,720
in this case the Sierra variety.

246
00:26:34,799 --> 00:26:37,240
Every year they march back
to their place of birth,

247
00:26:37,720 --> 00:26:40,000
ready to make more baby newts.

248
00:26:43,960 --> 00:26:46,559
Most amphibians
are nervous out in the open,

249
00:26:46,759 --> 00:26:49,079
but orange is a warning sign.

250
00:26:50,759 --> 00:26:55,480
This species has toxins
100 times more potent than cyanide.

251
00:26:55,720 --> 00:26:58,559
If a human ate one,
they'd drop dead.

252
00:27:08,079 --> 00:27:11,000
Like all of amphibian kind,
they breed in water.

253
00:27:11,799 --> 00:27:14,720
But at this pool party
anything goes.

254
00:27:23,519 --> 00:27:26,079
Boys on boys, boys on girls.

255
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:36,000
Salamander sex
is as colourful as their skin.

256
00:27:45,720 --> 00:27:47,720
But at least some get it right.

257
00:27:48,240 --> 00:27:51,720
Twigs in the pond are decorated
with sticky sacks of eggs.

258
00:27:57,000 --> 00:27:59,720
A European relative
is more reserved.

259
00:28:06,559 --> 00:28:10,720
The best party hat is worn
by the great crested newt.

260
00:28:16,240 --> 00:28:19,039
If a male can impress a female
with his outfit,

261
00:28:19,240 --> 00:28:22,480
then she'll allow him to pass over
a package of sperm.

262
00:28:25,720 --> 00:28:27,759
He'll drop it off
and she'll pick it up

263
00:28:28,079 --> 00:28:31,000
and use it to fertilise her eggs.

264
00:28:34,720 --> 00:28:38,000
The female folds a leaf
and lays an egg into the fold,

265
00:28:38,240 --> 00:28:41,000
its jelly
cementing the two halves together.

266
00:28:44,519 --> 00:28:47,960
It's a painstaking approach,
but it's the best she can do

267
00:28:48,039 --> 00:28:50,720
to hide her babies
from hungry mouths.

268
00:28:54,319 --> 00:28:57,240
Like all amphibians,
the babies need a makeover

269
00:28:57,319 --> 00:29:00,000
before they'll be ready
to breed themselves.

270
00:29:02,240 --> 00:29:03,799
It will take four months for them

271
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:06,720
to become smaller versions
of their parents.

272
00:29:10,319 --> 00:29:14,000
Though they're salamanders,
these newts aren't very toxic,

273
00:29:14,240 --> 00:29:16,960
but even their most deadly of kin
can't compete

274
00:29:17,039 --> 00:29:19,480
with a South American cousin.

275
00:29:25,720 --> 00:29:30,039
You might wonder what indigenous
hunters have to do with amphibians.

276
00:29:32,240 --> 00:29:36,240
In the dense Amazon rainforest,
they're hunting squirrel monkeys.

277
00:29:48,079 --> 00:29:51,720
They're using a blow gun,
with poison-tipped darts.

278
00:30:03,000 --> 00:30:05,279
It kills within seconds.

279
00:30:09,000 --> 00:30:10,519
And you've guessed it.

280
00:30:10,720 --> 00:30:14,519
The poison comes from
the aptly named poison dart frogs,

281
00:30:14,720 --> 00:30:17,480
tiny, colourful
rainforest inhabitants.

282
00:30:22,240 --> 00:30:24,720
The frogs are only a few
centimetres long,

283
00:30:24,960 --> 00:30:27,000
but don't underestimate
their potency.

284
00:30:27,240 --> 00:30:31,480
Some species contain enough toxin
to kill half a dozen people.

285
00:30:34,240 --> 00:30:36,960
Deadly as they are,
they're model parents.

286
00:30:39,079 --> 00:30:42,720
The Red Amazonica couple
will be faithful to each other,

287
00:30:42,799 --> 00:30:45,480
though it's dad
who does most of the parenting.

288
00:30:48,720 --> 00:30:52,240
The female's contribution is
to lay a few unfertilised eggs

289
00:30:52,480 --> 00:30:54,240
as food for her infants.

290
00:30:54,720 --> 00:30:56,759
But after that
she leaves them too it.

291
00:31:02,000 --> 00:31:04,480
Normally amphibians
lay eggs in water,

292
00:31:04,720 --> 00:31:07,240
but these must survive
in a blob of jelly.

293
00:31:09,240 --> 00:31:11,720
The male stands guard
until the eggs hatch,

294
00:31:12,000 --> 00:31:13,960
and then he offers them a lift.

295
00:31:16,000 --> 00:31:19,240
Instinctively,
a tadpole wriggles onto his back.

296
00:31:26,720 --> 00:31:29,319
And then he begins an epic journey.

297
00:31:39,759 --> 00:31:42,480
The rainforest has high humidity,

298
00:31:42,559 --> 00:31:47,480
but large bodies of water are hard
to find and teeming with predators.

299
00:31:53,000 --> 00:31:54,759
But he has a plan.

300
00:31:59,319 --> 00:32:02,720
He clambers skywards
until he sees a bromeliad.

301
00:32:04,480 --> 00:32:07,799
Bromeliads are not parasites,
but they grow on other plants

302
00:32:08,000 --> 00:32:10,240
to get them closer to sunlight.

303
00:32:15,480 --> 00:32:17,720
To live high up,
they have the useful habit

304
00:32:17,960 --> 00:32:19,720
of collecting rain water.

305
00:32:20,000 --> 00:32:21,319
It's to keep themselves wet,

306
00:32:21,519 --> 00:32:24,720
but in the process providing
hot tubs for tadpoles.

307
00:32:28,720 --> 00:32:31,319
Dad drops each baby
in its own plant pot,

308
00:32:31,519 --> 00:32:34,000
an aquatic nursery
where it can live in safety

309
00:32:34,240 --> 00:32:38,079
until it's ready to transform
into an adult frog and hop off.

310
00:32:43,480 --> 00:32:45,079
It's hard-core parenting,

311
00:32:45,279 --> 00:32:48,720
but there's another frog
that wins 'Father of the Year'.

312
00:32:55,079 --> 00:32:59,720
Huge rivers and wetlands of Africa
are subject to seasonal drying out.

313
00:33:06,000 --> 00:33:08,720
It leaves some residents
high and dry.

314
00:33:11,000 --> 00:33:15,240
Thousands of matchbox-sized
tadpoles and their beefy dad.

315
00:33:22,039 --> 00:33:25,480
He's on guard
and under submarine attack.

316
00:33:32,079 --> 00:33:35,799
It's another marauding male.
And dad's not happy.

317
00:33:55,559 --> 00:33:58,240
These heavy-weights,
football-sized frogs,

318
00:33:58,480 --> 00:34:02,480
will fight to the death
over who gets to keep the babies.

319
00:34:19,639 --> 00:34:21,599
Our devoted dad wins.

320
00:34:24,000 --> 00:34:26,599
He'll fearlessly defend his brood
for three weeks

321
00:34:26,920 --> 00:34:29,119
until their metamorphosis
is complete,

322
00:34:29,480 --> 00:34:31,599
but a lot can change in that time.

323
00:34:37,119 --> 00:34:39,360
The mercury is rising.

324
00:34:43,639 --> 00:34:46,360
The young
are halfway to becoming chowder.

325
00:34:53,480 --> 00:34:55,920
Dad must act fast.

326
00:35:00,519 --> 00:35:03,000
Instinct tells him what to do.

327
00:35:13,000 --> 00:35:15,480
He puts his powerful
hopping legs to work.

328
00:35:18,079 --> 00:35:20,079
He's a dambuster.

329
00:35:49,119 --> 00:35:52,639
The larger pool should keep them
going for a while.

330
00:35:58,119 --> 00:36:02,159
Even with a hero in the family, the
hoppers face a life of hardship.

331
00:36:08,000 --> 00:36:11,039
Few predators turn their nose up
at frog legs.

332
00:36:15,960 --> 00:36:19,920
For the common frog of Europe, the
grass snake is enemy number one.

333
00:36:35,920 --> 00:36:38,360
The water-loving reptile terrorises

334
00:36:38,519 --> 00:36:40,639
all the amphibians
of its wetland home.

335
00:36:53,480 --> 00:36:56,079
But it's important to look
before you leap.

336
00:37:10,199 --> 00:37:12,480
Frogs certainly
make a popular dish.

337
00:37:18,440 --> 00:37:22,480
Even bears aren't above a bit of
weight-lifting to sample the goods.

338
00:37:32,480 --> 00:37:36,039
Damp rocky hideouts
equal a salamander buffet.

339
00:37:47,639 --> 00:37:50,000
The bears
seem to instinctively know

340
00:37:50,119 --> 00:37:53,679
which kinds are the least toxic
before they chow down.

341
00:38:02,000 --> 00:38:03,639
There are dangers everywhere.

342
00:38:03,960 --> 00:38:07,519
But this African red toad
thinks he's found a safe house.

343
00:38:09,599 --> 00:38:11,920
But he's not the first
to shelter here.

344
00:38:17,199 --> 00:38:19,079
A deadly spitting cobra.

345
00:38:40,000 --> 00:38:43,199
It looks like toad in the hole,
but it's not over yet.

346
00:38:45,480 --> 00:38:49,000
If all else fails, the toad
has another trick up his sleeve.

347
00:38:53,159 --> 00:38:57,480
He inflates his body so much so
that even the hungry snake

348
00:38:57,679 --> 00:39:00,039
can't get its mouth around him.

349
00:39:11,000 --> 00:39:14,079
Amazing amphibians
use every trick in the book

350
00:39:14,400 --> 00:39:16,480
to survive a dangerous world.

351
00:39:19,400 --> 00:39:22,880
But poisons,
hopping and puffing are nothing.

352
00:39:23,000 --> 00:39:25,679
Meet the masters of disguise.

353
00:39:28,480 --> 00:39:31,480
Leaf-cutter ants work tirelessly
to gather vegetation

354
00:39:31,639 --> 00:39:34,920
to take back to their nests,
building a food store.

355
00:39:50,519 --> 00:39:53,480
And it's not surprising
that after putting so much work

356
00:39:53,559 --> 00:39:57,000
into their larder,
they'll fiercely defend it.

357
00:39:58,480 --> 00:40:01,440
Trespassers are not welcome.

358
00:40:09,000 --> 00:40:11,480
And yet there's a frog inside.

359
00:40:13,199 --> 00:40:16,480
Any other frog in here
would find itself under attack,

360
00:40:16,639 --> 00:40:18,599
but not this one.

361
00:40:19,960 --> 00:40:24,079
Ants recognise each other,
not by sight but by scent,

362
00:40:24,400 --> 00:40:26,880
the chemical signals
they all give off.

363
00:40:31,519 --> 00:40:36,480
The frog has developed
its own unique cologne, eau de ant.

364
00:40:40,679 --> 00:40:45,119
In the cool damp basement of the
ant nest it can raise its young,

365
00:40:45,440 --> 00:40:49,000
protected by the fearsome ants
like a member of the family.

366
00:40:50,880 --> 00:40:55,000
But there are other creepy crawlies
you really don't want to mess with.

367
00:41:01,000 --> 00:41:03,079
The tarantula
is an intimidating predator

368
00:41:03,480 --> 00:41:07,559
that stalks the rainforest
for any small animals she can eat.

369
00:41:13,960 --> 00:41:16,119
A spider cave protects her babies

370
00:41:16,440 --> 00:41:18,880
until they're as scary
as their mother.

371
00:41:23,119 --> 00:41:25,400
It's not a place
for the faint-hearted.

372
00:41:34,000 --> 00:41:37,000
A frog has found its way
into her lair.

373
00:41:58,639 --> 00:42:03,480
But he survives. Again,
his pheromones reassure the spider.

374
00:42:12,480 --> 00:42:14,000
He's a welcome lodger,

375
00:42:14,159 --> 00:42:19,039
some reports even suggest
a spider keeps a frog as a pet,

376
00:42:20,000 --> 00:42:22,519
though in fact
it's a mutual arrangement.

377
00:42:25,960 --> 00:42:28,480
Big scary spider
protects her little frog,

378
00:42:28,840 --> 00:42:31,440
and the amphibian pays its rent
by eating ants

379
00:42:31,599 --> 00:42:35,679
and other predatory insects that
might harm her baby spiderlings.

380
00:42:53,119 --> 00:42:56,480
It appears that amphibians
have found ingenious solutions

381
00:42:56,679 --> 00:42:59,480
to almost any obstacle,
and as a result,

382
00:42:59,639 --> 00:43:02,000
their kingdom knows no bounds.

383
00:43:06,119 --> 00:43:09,880
Humid tropical rainforest
looks like frog-heaven,

384
00:43:10,000 --> 00:43:12,480
but not here atop a mountain.

385
00:43:17,440 --> 00:43:20,000
But melting glaciers form pools

386
00:43:20,119 --> 00:43:23,159
and potential
for one intrepid amphibian.

387
00:43:29,000 --> 00:43:34,039
The Siberian salamander can cope
with minus 45 degrees centigrade.

388
00:43:42,000 --> 00:43:44,480
They've even be known
to survive the odd winter,

389
00:43:44,639 --> 00:43:47,360
frozen solid in permafrost.

390
00:43:52,840 --> 00:43:56,480
But the weirdest of them all
lives inside the mountain itself.

391
00:44:00,000 --> 00:44:02,159
In limestone karsts under the Alps

392
00:44:02,480 --> 00:44:05,679
lurks Europe's only
cave-dwelling vertebrate.

393
00:44:07,039 --> 00:44:11,519
Legend records it as a baby dragon,
living in the caves,

394
00:44:11,840 --> 00:44:14,840
slowly growing
into a fearsome beast.

395
00:44:21,039 --> 00:44:24,480
The strangest of specialists,
the olm.

396
00:44:26,400 --> 00:44:29,079
It only reaches
30 centimetres long.

397
00:44:29,480 --> 00:44:33,480
Like most young amphibians,
it has feathery external gills,

398
00:44:33,639 --> 00:44:35,960
but they stay with it
throughout life.

399
00:44:37,119 --> 00:44:41,000
It's blind and finds its food
with hearing and smell.

400
00:44:42,440 --> 00:44:45,119
Some local people
call it the human fish

401
00:44:45,440 --> 00:44:49,000
because of its ghostly
pigmentless skin.

402
00:44:52,960 --> 00:44:57,360
Living underground in perpetual
darkness it has no need for colour.

403
00:45:02,000 --> 00:45:05,440
Amazing amphibians
have certainly conquered the globe.

404
00:45:12,000 --> 00:45:15,000
But the world still throws
challenges at them.

405
00:45:42,039 --> 00:45:44,480
When winter arrives
in northern forests,

406
00:45:44,960 --> 00:45:49,079
waterways are brought
to a standstill, the ground freezes

407
00:45:49,400 --> 00:45:52,679
and so do the bodies
of any animals trapped within.

408
00:46:17,480 --> 00:46:21,119
It's good for nature.
Germs and bacteria will be killed,

409
00:46:21,440 --> 00:46:24,480
seeds will be protected
until they grow in spring,

410
00:46:24,599 --> 00:46:28,559
but for many individuals
this is the end of the road.

411
00:46:38,360 --> 00:46:41,199
But amphibians
have one last magic trick

412
00:46:41,480 --> 00:46:44,360
and it's one
of biblical proportions.

413
00:46:53,679 --> 00:46:56,079
Spring sunshine
slowly unlocks the forest

414
00:46:56,400 --> 00:46:59,119
from months in suspended animation.

415
00:47:13,480 --> 00:47:17,000
Under the leaf-litter,
the iced-up body of a frog.

416
00:47:17,199 --> 00:47:20,000
Even his eyes
are solid balls of ice.

417
00:47:28,840 --> 00:47:33,000
But as the ice melts
the dead seem to rise again.

418
00:47:36,000 --> 00:47:38,519
First his heart
begins to gently beat,

419
00:47:38,840 --> 00:47:41,559
attempting to kick-start
his circulation.

420
00:47:44,000 --> 00:47:48,199
Blood reaches his brain.
It's like the on-switch.

421
00:47:52,000 --> 00:47:54,119
His liver
produced an oily antifreeze

422
00:47:54,440 --> 00:47:56,400
which was pumped around his body.

423
00:47:56,480 --> 00:48:00,960
So although the water in his body
froze, his cells did not.

424
00:48:01,039 --> 00:48:03,400
They just had to wait for the thaw.

425
00:48:07,480 --> 00:48:10,480
A big of wriggling to warm up,
and he's ready to hop.

426
00:48:12,000 --> 00:48:15,119
Frogs can shut down
for eight months of the year,

427
00:48:15,440 --> 00:48:18,039
but it doesn't damage
their zest for life.

428
00:48:21,960 --> 00:48:24,480
And to make the most
of a fleeting northern spring,

429
00:48:24,880 --> 00:48:26,920
there's work to be done.

430
00:48:28,960 --> 00:48:31,440
Amphibians
are almost irrepressible.

431
00:48:31,519 --> 00:48:34,519
They've hopped and croaked through
hundreds of millions of years

432
00:48:34,840 --> 00:48:37,480
of harsh weather
and hungry predators.

433
00:48:39,480 --> 00:48:44,000
Now there's a new threat,
chytridiomycosis,

434
00:48:44,480 --> 00:48:48,440
a virus spread by fungi that kills
frogs by stopping their ability

435
00:48:48,519 --> 00:48:51,480
to control their body salts
and moisture levels.

436
00:48:55,039 --> 00:48:58,000
It's already wiped out 200 species.

437
00:48:59,079 --> 00:49:01,039
It's an endless struggle onwards.

438
00:49:02,079 --> 00:49:06,079
But the world over, scientists are
trying to find a way to help.

439
00:49:07,639 --> 00:49:10,960
They won't allow the frog family
to croak its last.

440
00:49:12,679 --> 00:49:15,519
And coupled with the frogs'
enthusiasm for breeding,

441
00:49:15,840 --> 00:49:19,000
there's every chance
they can bounce back.

